# ─── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# LF in the repo AND in every working tree, on every platform.
#
# `eol=lf` is the load-bearing half. Git for Windows ships
# core.autocrlf=true, so a clone on C: checks out CRLF -- and the SAME clone
# read from WSL then shows every file modified. Measured: 130 files, 26,602
# insertions and 26,602 deletions, which is every line of the repo. Nothing was
# edited; the two gits simply disagreed about what the file says.
#
# This is not only cosmetic. Everything in lib/, install.sh and */deps.sh runs
# under bash, and a CRLF shebang line or a CRLF `fi` is a syntax error -- the
# WSL side would break the moment it ran anything out of that clone.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` then overrides autocrlf in
# both directions, so this holds without asking anyone to configure their git.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so no normalization is ever attempted on them.
*.ttf binary
*.png binary
*.zip binary

# claude/settings.json is written by Claude Code at runtime, through the symlink
# declared in deps.conf's [claude] section. model and effortLevel are per-machine
# and per-conversation preferences, so they are stripped on the way into git by
# the clean filter in tools/clean-claude-settings.sh.
#
# The filter itself is registered by claude/deps.sh: filter.* config lives in
# .git/config and cannot be committed, so this line alone does nothing until
# `make install claude` has run. That is the safe direction -- an unregistered
# filter is silently skipped rather than erroring.
claude/settings.json filter=claude-settings
